	Here's a thought.  We know that a networked nethack would be totally
out of control.  Splitting all the named weapons (since only one can exist),
all the food, and having one character stealing the shops blind, leaving
nothing behind, would make the game totally impossible.
	If a networked nethack were to be created, perhaps the right way to
do it would be to have the game randomly tie games together.  What I am
proposing is something like this:
	Character A descends onto Level X.  Character B has been playing on
Level X for some time now.  The game chooses to network the games together.
So, for Level X ONLY, they are tied together.  When Character B descends, the
game continues his dungeon as if nothing had happened.  Same for Character A.
	So, while the game would be "networked" it would not affect the
overall gameplay for the entire thing.
	Of course, when you reach the Castle level, networking is no longer
possible.
	The one danger is that in Character A's universe, artifacts might
show up more than once.  For instance, if Character B is holding Excalibur,
and Character A runs into another network player Character C.  Character C
has not come into contact with Character B, so his game has generated his own
Excalibur.  When Character A runs into C, then in A's universe, there are two
Excalibur's in existence.
	Actually, an interesting background would be to make the "ocean"
level be the only level with network abilities, which would make it similar
to Moorcock's "Sailor on the Seas of Fate" where many eternal champions meet
one another.

